WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.350
Scientists think they've made the first
measurements from the cosmic dawn the

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:08.730
period in the Universe's history when
the first stars began to shine. But how

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:12.450
can scientists study something that
happened more than thirteen and a half

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:19.100
billion years ago? Well to understand
that you need to start with hydrogen.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:24.210
Hydrogen is a cosmologist's best friend
they use it to study everything. You see

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:29.269
a quirk of hydrogen is that it can emit
photons as it switches between states

00:00:29.269 --> 00:00:34.559
specifically photons with a wavelength
at around 21 centimeters. When

00:00:34.559 --> 00:00:38.460
astronomers point radio telescopes at
the sky they see a mess of different

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:43.680
radio waves but nestled in there is
always that 21 centimeter line. These

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:47.700
emissions from hydrogen have helped
scientists to map the universe. It's how

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:51.600
they discovered that we're sitting in a
huge spiral-shaped galaxy the Milky Way.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.670
But hydrogen isn't just useful for
studying the Universe today. Right after

00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:01.620
the Big Bang the Universe was a dark
soup of electrons and protons. The

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:05.760
particles soon clumped together as
hydrogen and after hundreds of millions

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.240
of years
hydrogen collected into stars, generating

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:14.280
light. Cosmologists call this the
cosmic dawn. That primordial light is

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:18.540
hard to see with a telescope because the
tiny amounts of light made by the first

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:22.500
stars would be extremely faint. So
scientists have taken a slightly

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:27.530
different tack. You see when that light
was first created it changed everything

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:33.150
including the hydrogen itself crucially
making it able to absorb radiation at

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:38.150
its favorite 21 centimeter line. With
this in mind rather than look for

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:44.250
emission from hydrogen cosmologists have
looked instead for its absorption. They

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:48.270
turned to the Cosmic Microwave
Background the mass of radiation left

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:53.820
over from the Big Bang. In theory the
moment light hit that early hydrogen it

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:57.960
would have started absorbing this
radiation and that should have left a

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.430
blip in the CMB.
And that after decades of scouring for

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:07.229
evidence from this time and two years of
verification is what cosmologists have

00:02:07.229 --> 00:02:10.520
foun. But here's where it gets
interesting

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:14.960
the signal they found is not at 21
centimeters anymore because space itself

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:21.110
is expanding that line has stretched
over time which is handy for us because

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:26.060
the amount of stretching the wavelength
for light is it now tells us how long

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:30.800
the light has traveled and so when those
stars formed. Finally this allows

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.060
scientists to put some dates to the
beginning of the cosmic dawn but that isn't

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:38.690
all. While the time the first stars formed
seems about right

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:43.130
the signal is way bigger than
cosmologists predicted. A lot more

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:46.910
radiation was absorbed from this cosmic
microwave background that makes sense

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:51.290
given what we know so far about the
early Universe. Something doesn't add up

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:57.140
and one tantalizing possibility is that
we may be seeing the influence of dark

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:03.470
matter. Now this is far from confirmed
but if true it would give us a wealth of

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:07.490
information about dark matter's
properties and where to look for it. So

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:11.840
this little blip could not only give us
a snapshot of the first stars, but it

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:17.890
could be the key to unlocking one of the
biggest mysteries in physics

